  "subtitles": [
    {
      "id": "passage-01-02-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 0,
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 5.542,
      "text": "The Map of Observing the Mysteries of the Heaven and Earth (Liangyi xuanlan tu 兩儀玄覽圖),"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-01-02-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 0,
      "start": 5.542,
      "end": 11.479041666666667,
      "text": "which we call here the Xuantu, is a worldmap in eight panels created in 1603"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-01-02-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 0,
      "start": 11.479041666666667,
      "end": 16.916666666666668,
      "text": "by the Ming dynasty military official Li Yingshi李應試."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-01-03-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 1,
      "start": 16.916666666666668,
      "end": 21.776333666666666,
      "text": "There is strong reason to believe that the Manchus obtained this particular map before"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-01-03-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 1,
      "start": 21.776333666666666,
      "end": 27.093333666666666,
      "text": "they conquered China, a surprising development that makes us reconsider the Manchus'"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-01-03-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 1,
      "start": 27.093333666666666,
      "end": 30.291666666666668,
      "text": "relationship to Western cartography."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-02-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 2,
      "start": 30.291666666666668,
      "end": 36.50052083333334,
      "text": "Matteo Ricci and the scholar Li Zhizao 李之藻 created in 1602 a map named Complete"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-02-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 2,
      "start": 36.50052083333334,
      "end": 40.807208333333335,
      "text": "Map of the Myriad Kingdoms of the World (Kunyu Wanguo Quantu 坤輿萬國全圖)."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-02-01-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 2,
      "start": 40.807208333333335,
      "end": 46.008208333333336,
      "text": "It included geographical descriptions of the historical five continents, complete with"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-02-01-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 2,
      "start": 46.008208333333336,
      "end": 52.38220833333334,
      "text": "place-names, a précis of Ptolemaic cosmology, and paper instruments for measuring polar"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-02-01-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 2,
      "start": 52.38220833333334,
      "end": 54.875,
      "text": "altitude and latitude."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-03-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 3,
      "start": 54.875,
      "end": 61.852708666666665,
      "text": "The Xuantu is a large object, measuring 2 meters vertically and 4.42 meters"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-03-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 3,
      "start": 61.852708666666665,
      "end": 67.60470866666667,
      "text": "horizontally. Maps of this size meant for a high-end audience of officials were rarely"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-03-01-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 3,
      "start": 67.60470866666667,
      "end": 74.07070866666666,
      "text": "prints. Instead, just as for paintings, Chinese artisans normally used silk, which was"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-03-01-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 3,
      "start": 74.07070866666666,
      "end": 77.375,
      "text": "favored for its superior durability."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-04-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 4,
      "start": 77.375,
      "end": 83.372333,
      "text": "For Sino-Western world maps, the desire to quickly disseminate many copies trumped that"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-04-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 4,
      "start": 83.372333,
      "end": 89.16733309246175,
      "text": "of creating solid, durable objects. They were as large in size as silk maps made"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-04-01-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 4,
      "start": 89.16733309246175,
      "end": 94.19433324424315,
      "text": "for important people, yet easily reproducible and affordable."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-04-01-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 4,
      "start": 94.19433324424315,
      "end": 97.9682499867785,
      "text": "This was at the root of their success in the late Ming."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-04-01-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 4,
      "start": 97.9682499867785,
      "end": 101.95024977130232,
      "text": "It is also the reason why so few copies survived:"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-04-01-subtitle-06",
      "passage": 4,
      "start": 101.95024977130232,
      "end": 104.875,
      "text": "large prints were fragile."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 5,
      "start": 104.875,
      "end": 109.43804166666666,
      "text": "Indeed, Xuantu is important not so much for what it has in it but for"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 5,
      "start": 109.43804166666666,
      "end": 115.91666666666667,
      "text": "what it has on it: the repairs, modifications, and annotations added to the original print."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-03-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 6,
      "start": 115.91666666666667,
      "end": 119.27699966666667,
      "text": "The Shenyang copy was originally uncolored."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-03-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 6,
      "start": 119.27699966666667,
      "end": 124.81499966666667,
      "text": "Yet during its life in the Shenyang palace, an artisan was tasked with embellishing the"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-03-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 6,
      "start": 124.81499966666667,
      "end": 130.35299966666668,
      "text": "mountain ranges across all the continents in the style of Chinese painting."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-03-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 6,
      "start": 130.35299966666668,
      "end": 136.09999966666666,
      "text": "The color scheme of these decorations, blue, green, and gold, fits the one used"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-03-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 6,
      "start": 136.09999966666666,
      "end": 138.75,
      "text": "throughout the Shenyang palace."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-04-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 7,
      "start": 138.75,
      "end": 144.317667,
      "text": "Besides the repairs and added decorative elements, the Shenyang copy of the Map of"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-04-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 7,
      "start": 144.317667,
      "end": 148.534667,
      "text": "Observing Mysteries displays a number of Manchu annotations."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-04-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 7,
      "start": 148.534667,
      "end": 153.585667,
      "text": "The theory of the map expounded in the preface by Matteo Ricci presented relationships"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-04-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 7,
      "start": 153.585667,
      "end": 159.05366700000002,
      "text": "between Earth's spherical shape, its position in the solar system, and the cycles of"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-04-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 7,
      "start": 159.05366700000002,
      "end": 164.104667,
      "text": "night and day. All of these features, represented by lines on the map, had Manchu"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-04-subtitle-06",
      "passage": 7,
      "start": 164.104667,
      "end": 166.58333333333334,
      "text": "translations attached."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-07-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 8,
      "start": 166.58333333333334,
      "end": 172.31700033333334,
      "text": "Taken together, they suggest that the Manchu translator had a grasp of and an interest"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-07-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 8,
      "start": 172.31700033333334,
      "end": 179.375,
      "text": "in the “theory” of the map, having read and understood the prefaces and other accompanying texts."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-08-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 9,
      "start": 179.375,
      "end": 183.99237497958404,
      "text": "When it was used in the Shenyang palace, the Xuantu was damaged."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-08-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 9,
      "start": 183.99237497958404,
      "end": 188.1953749077379,
      "text": "Winters in Shenyang are bitterly cold, and rooms must be heated."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-08-01-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 9,
      "start": 188.1953749077379,
      "end": 192.68837483093446,
      "text": "For this reason, there was a temperature difference between the upper and lower parts of"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-08-01-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 9,
      "start": 192.68837483093446,
      "end": 197.94737474103704,
      "text": "the room. This created condensation, and because the map was most likely displayed"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-08-01-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 9,
      "start": 197.94737474103704,
      "end": 203.29166666666666,
      "text": "vertically, the dampness damaged the portions closer to the ground."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-08-02-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 10,
      "start": 203.29166666666666,
      "end": 208.81033366666665,
      "text": "An artisan had applied paper paste and, after it dried, redrew the meridians to give"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-08-02-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 10,
      "start": 208.81033366666665,
      "end": 214.45233366666665,
      "text": "the impression of continuity. Further damage to the map reveals the discrepancy between"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-08-02-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 10,
      "start": 214.45233366666665,
      "end": 216.33333333333334,
      "text": "the two layers."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-09-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 11,
      "start": 216.33333333333334,
      "end": 221.04533333333333,
      "text": "The amount of information included in the reconstruction on the top stratum differed"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-09-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 11,
      "start": 221.04533333333333,
      "end": 222.98433333333335,
      "text": "from that of the layer below."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-09-01-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 11,
      "start": 222.98433333333335,
      "end": 226.65233333333333,
      "text": "This was due perhaps to the artisan's limited knowledge."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-09-01-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 11,
      "start": 226.65233333333333,
      "end": 230.94833333333335,
      "text": "But it might also be a reflection of the interests of the map owners."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-09-01-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 11,
      "start": 230.94833333333335,
      "end": 236.15033333333335,
      "text": "The Southern Hemisphere on the left side of the map (roll one), for instance,"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-09-01-subtitle-06",
      "passage": 11,
      "start": 236.15033333333335,
      "end": 240.14433333333335,
      "text": "disappeared completely, being covered up in the restoration;"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-09-01-subtitle-07",
      "passage": 11,
      "start": 240.14433333333335,
      "end": 245.99433333333334,
      "text": "only a circle and some of the radiating meridians were copied on top for consistency."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-09-01-subtitle-08",
      "passage": 11,
      "start": 245.99433333333334,
      "end": 251.23133333333334,
      "text": "On the other hand, the northern hemisphere was preserved, and annotations were added to"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-09-01-subtitle-09",
      "passage": 11,
      "start": 251.23133333333334,
      "end": 256.0,
      "text": "it because it encompassed places the Manchus were interested in."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 12,
      "start": 256.0,
      "end": 261.965,
      "text": "Most translations of place-names are concentrated in East Asia, radiating toward Europe."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 12,
      "start": 261.965,
      "end": 267.259,
      "text": "The Manchus, it seems, were primarily preoccupied with locating themselves and their"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-01-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 12,
      "start": 267.259,
      "end": 274.125,
      "text": "immediate surroundings; in terms of their outward interest, they focused on looking westward."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-02-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 13,
      "start": 274.125,
      "end": 278.2613122140055,
      "text": "The translation of Mount Changbai (Changbai Shan長白山) is more revealing of the Manchus'"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-02-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 13,
      "start": 278.2613122140055,
      "end": 282.61531232686195,
      "text": "own emerging sense of identity in the sixteenth century."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-02-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 13,
      "start": 282.61531232686195,
      "end": 288.2453124727925,
      "text": "The mountain, appearing here in translation as Sanggiyan Alin, was part of an imperial"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-02-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 13,
      "start": 288.2453124727925,
      "end": 293.18966666666665,
      "text": "cult throughout the Qing, revered as the birthplace of the mythical founder of the"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-02-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 13,
      "start": 293.18966666666665,
      "end": 299.00666666666666,
      "text": "dynasty, Bukūri Yongšon. The addition of an inscription alongside the mountain is an"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-02-subtitle-06",
      "passage": 13,
      "start": 299.00666666666666,
      "end": 305.125,
      "text": "indication that the place was meaningful for the Manchus as early as the Later Jin."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-03-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 14,
      "start": 305.125,
      "end": 310.729,
      "text": "Moving beyond this sphere, immediately neighboring countries such as Japan, Korea, and"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-03-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 14,
      "start": 310.729,
      "end": 315.15291666666667,
      "text": "the Great Ming are labeled using names of Manchu origin:"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-03-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 14,
      "start": 315.15291666666667,
      "end": 324.2083333333333,
      "text": "Ose, Sohlo, and Nikan. These names do not describe places or countries but, rather, ethnicities."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-04-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 15,
      "start": 324.2083333333333,
      "end": 330.1590003333333,
      "text": "The rest of the translations of place-names on the map are transliterations in Manchu of"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-04-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 15,
      "start": 330.1590003333333,
      "end": 335.5150003333333,
      "text": "the pronunciation of Chinese characters. The selection of these places cannot be easily"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-04-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 15,
      "start": 335.5150003333333,
      "end": 340.0416666666667,
      "text": "explained by what we readily know about the Manchus."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-11-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 16,
      "start": 340.0416666666667,
      "end": 345.2423336666667,
      "text": "The translations of places like the Fortunate Islands, the Cape of Good Hope,"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-11-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 16,
      "start": 345.2423336666667,
      "end": 350.6413336666667,
      "text": "Madagascar, Java, and the Philippines resulted from an awareness of the centers of the"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-11-01-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 16,
      "start": 350.6413336666667,
      "end": 357.3403336666667,
      "text": "European trade network around Eurasia. These were, after all, places from which ships"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-11-01-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 16,
      "start": 357.3403336666667,
      "end": 361.0416666666667,
      "text": "dropping anchor at Macao would have been coming."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 17,
      "start": 361.0416666666667,
      "end": 365.91599966666666,
      "text": "According to the Manchu Veritable Records, the Manchu written language was a direct"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 17,
      "start": 365.91599966666666,
      "end": 372.13166666666666,
      "text": "invention of bilingual scholars working for Nurhaci and was established in 1599."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-01-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 17,
      "start": 372.13166666666666,
      "end": 379.25,
      "text": "The script could not clearly distinguish a and e, o and u, or k, g, and h."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-02-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 18,
      "start": 379.25,
      "end": 384.442,
      "text": "A reform of the language that would address these issues was implemented during the time"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-02-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 18,
      "start": 384.442,
      "end": 390.34,
      "text": "of the second Khan, Hong Taiji. The most important and visible aspect of this reform"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-02-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 18,
      "start": 390.34,
      "end": 395.625,
      "text": "was the addition of dots and circles to solve the problem of distinction."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-03-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 19,
      "start": 395.625,
      "end": 400.116333,
      "text": "Analysis of the form of the script on the map thus indicates that the Map"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-03-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 19,
      "start": 400.116333,
      "end": 407.256333,
      "text": "of Observing Mysteries was translated between roughly 1625 and 1636, when the reformed"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-03-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 19,
      "start": 407.256333,
      "end": 410.472333,
      "text": "version of the Old Manchu script was used."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-03-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 19,
      "start": 410.472333,
      "end": 414.906333,
      "text": "In any case, the map's annotations indicate an interest in, as well as an"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-03-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 19,
      "start": 414.906333,
      "end": 421.547333,
      "text": "epistemological engagement with, a Sino-Western artifact in a crucial moment, as the"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-03-subtitle-06",
      "passage": 19,
      "start": 421.547333,
      "end": 425.75,
      "text": "Manchus were beginning to plan their conquest of China."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-13-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 20,
      "start": 425.75,
      "end": 430.59924993692863,
      "text": "This section of the map of the Great Ming depicts the western portion of Korea"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-13-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 20,
      "start": 430.59924993692863,
      "end": 435.8232498061979,
      "text": "and displays the Manchu place-name transliterations on affixed labels."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-13-01-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 20,
      "start": 435.8232498061979,
      "end": 442.375,
      "text": "In some places, over time, the labels have detached, revealing the original Chinese text underneath."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-14-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 21,
      "start": 442.375,
      "end": 448.25033314881796,
      "text": "Repairs, modifications, as well as annotations therefore, all belonged to a broad"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-14-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 21,
      "start": 448.25033314881796,
      "end": 453.92833329263766,
      "text": "strategy of translation of cartographic artifacts first practiced in the preconquest"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-14-01-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 21,
      "start": 453.92833329263766,
      "end": 459.9203950555557,
      "text": "period on the map by Li Yingshi and Ricci, and ultimately, after the conquest, on"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-14-01-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 21,
      "start": 459.9203950555557,
      "end": 467.0,
      "text": "Ming maps. This demonstrates the continuity in translation across the Ming-Qing divide."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-14-02-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 22,
      "start": 467.0,
      "end": 472.206,
      "text": "The presence of a map co-authored by Matteo Ricci in the Shenyang palace is ultimately"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-14-02-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 22,
      "start": 472.206,
      "end": 480.0,
      "text": "a testament to the extent of knowledge networks in the early modern period."
    }
  ],
